WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.640
Multi-turn conversation design is what separates a single prompt from an actual dialogue with the model,

2
00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:10.560
and the choice between them shapes the entire interaction.

3
00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:17.040
In this video, we'll cover when a dialogue beats a single prompt, how to design classification turns,

4
00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.680
breaking complex tasks into turn sequences,

5
00:00:19.968 --> 00:00:22.528
and the iterative refinement pattern.

6
00:00:22.528 --> 00:00:27.248
Some tasks fall apart the moment you try to cram them into one message,

7
00:00:27.248 --> 00:00:31.288
and recognizing those tasks early saves a lot of rework.

8
00:00:31.288 --> 00:00:35.488
A multi-turn dialogue is a conversation across two or more exchanges

9
00:00:35.488 --> 00:00:39.888
where each turn builds on the prior response. It's the architecture you

10
00:00:39.936 --> 00:00:45.616
reach for when the task is too complex or ambiguous for a single call to handle well.

11
00:00:59.904 --> 00:01:05.584
exchanges that breaks a complex task into discrete steps, where each turn handles one

12
00:01:05.584 --> 00:01:09.224
part and passes context forward to the next.

13
00:01:09.224 --> 00:01:15.024
Together, these ideas describe the core toolkit for designing effective multi-turn interactions.

14
00:01:15.024 --> 00:01:19.864
Multi-turn dialogue outperforms a single prompt in four particular steps.

15
00:01:19.872 --> 00:01:26.832
First, when the task is ambiguous and any assumption the model makes could lead to wrong output,

16
00:01:26.832 --> 00:01:28.472
that then needs rework.

17
00:01:28.472 --> 00:01:34.872
Second, when the desired final output depends on intermediate results the model produces along the way.

18
00:01:34.872 --> 00:01:39.872
Third, when a human needs to review and approve one step before the next begins, because

19
00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:42.940
Because that review step breaks the flow of a single call.

20
00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:48.540
Fourth, when the task is long enough that fitting everything into one call creates context window

21
00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:52.780
pressure and risks degraded quality near the end.

22
00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:58.660
When any one of these conditions exists, dialogue is the stronger architectural choice.

23
00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:03.540
The comparison between a single call and a single call is a very simple and simple

24
00:01:59.808 --> 00:02:03.768
single long prompt and a multi-turn dialogue is worth making explicit.

25
00:02:03.768 --> 00:02:08.928
A single long prompt gives the model everything at once, which works well for well-defined

26
00:02:08.928 --> 00:02:12.088
tasks where the inputs are complete and clear.

27
00:02:12.088 --> 00:02:17.148
The problem is that single prompts fail badly, when inputs are ambiguous, when outputs are

28
00:02:17.148 --> 00:02:22.148
interdependent across steps, or when inputs are ambiguous, or when inputs are ambiguous,

29
00:02:19.776 --> 00:02:24.256
win the task benefits from a human review midway through.

30
00:02:24.256 --> 00:02:27.856
Multi-turn dialogue addresses all of those failure modes.

31
00:02:27.856 --> 00:02:30.656
Each turn can be smaller and more focused.

32
00:02:30.656 --> 00:02:33.176
You can course correct after each step.

33
00:02:33.176 --> 00:02:37.856
You can inject clarifications before a wrong assumption compounds.

34
00:02:37.856 --> 00:02:39.736
That flexibility is the real advantage

35
00:02:39.744 --> 00:02:42.384
dialogue over a monolithic prompt.

36
00:02:42.384 --> 00:02:45.984
A good clarification turn is disciplined.

37
00:02:45.984 --> 00:02:50.664
It starts by acknowledging what the model understood from the user's input.

38
00:02:50.664 --> 00:02:54.784
So the user knows their message was processed correctly.

39
00:02:54.784 --> 00:02:59.624
Then it identifies the single ambiguity that is most likely to produce wrong

40
00:02:59.712 --> 00:03:05.412
output, and asks exactly one targeted question about that ambiguity. It ends by setting

41
00:03:05.412 --> 00:03:11.272
an expectation for what comes next, once the question is answered. Asking one question

42
00:03:11.272 --> 00:03:16.472
rather than a list is critical. A clarification turn with three or four questions feels like

43
00:03:16.472 --> 00:03:19.672
an intake form rather than a conversation.

44
00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:25.200
users start to feel interrogated rather than helped. Pick the ambiguity that matters most

45
00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:28.080
and ask about only that one.

46
00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:33.000
The pitfalls in clarification turn design are predictable. Asking too many questions

47
00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:38.600
at once is the most common failure. Three or four open questions in a single turn create

48
00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:39.680
a form-filling experience.

49
00:03:39.648 --> 00:03:45.948
experience, not a dialogue. Clarifying things the model could reasonably infer from context,

50
00:03:45.948 --> 00:03:51.628
wastes a turn, and signals poor judgment to the user. Skipping acknowledgement of what

51
00:03:51.628 --> 00:03:57.508
was understood and jumping straight to a question makes users feel ignored. And open-ended

52
00:03:57.508 --> 00:03:59.628
fishing questions like "Can you tell me where you're going?"

53
00:03:59.616 --> 00:04:06.736
me more. Generate rambling responses rather than the specific detail the model actually needs.

54
00:04:06.736 --> 00:04:13.056
Good clarification turns our short, specific, and respectful of the user's time. Each of these

55
00:04:13.056 --> 00:04:19.456
pitfalls shares the same root, which is failing to treat the clarification as a purposeful,

56
00:04:19.584 --> 00:04:24.584
targeted exchange rather than a data gathering exercise.

57
00:04:24.584 --> 00:04:31.064
Breaking a complex task into a turn sequence starts with identifying the output dependencies.

58
00:04:31.064 --> 00:04:35.584
Ask what each step needs from the prior step before it can proceed.

59
00:04:35.584 --> 00:04:39.564
That dependency map becomes your turn structure. one clear goal.

60
00:04:39.552 --> 00:04:44.792
per turn, rather than bundling multiple objectives into a single message.

61
00:04:44.792 --> 00:04:49.312
When you send a turn, use the model's prior output as explicit input.

62
00:04:49.312 --> 00:04:55.152
"Or reference it, rather than assuming the model remembers the relevant detail."

63
00:04:55.152 --> 00:04:58.992
Keep each turn's instructions short and limited to the current step only.

64
00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:04.840
That focus makes each turn easier to evaluate and makes it straightforward to steer when

65
00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:10.240
a step goes wrong, rather than unwinding a long compounded error.

66
00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:16.720
Iterative Refinement is one of the most reliable turn sequence patterns for producing polished output.

67
00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:19.420
The first turn produces a draft based on the initial

68
00:05:19.488 --> 00:05:26.488
specification. The second turn delivers targeted feedback on specific aspects of the draft.

69
00:05:26.488 --> 00:05:32.028
Not a general "make this better" instruction, but concrete direction. Like, the opening

70
00:05:32.028 --> 00:05:37.308
paragraph is too abstract, rewrite it to lead with the main finding. The third turn

71
00:05:37.308 --> 00:05:39.308
applies revisions and can

72
00:05:39.456 --> 00:05:42.496
firms that the changes were made as requested.

73
00:05:42.496 --> 00:05:47.296
Subsequent turns addressed remaining issues one category at a time.

74
00:05:47.296 --> 00:05:53.296
This works because small, targeted changes are easier to verify than large rewrites,

75
00:05:53.296 --> 00:05:58.576
and each cycle brings the output closer to the target without losing what was already working.

76
00:05:59.424 --> 00:06:04.424
State signaling between turns keeps the model oriented in a long workflow.

77
00:06:04.424 --> 00:06:11.190
Opening a turn by summarizing the progress made in prior turns resets the model's working context to

78
00:06:11.190 --> 00:06:13.356
the right point in the workflow.

79
00:06:13.424 --> 00:06:19.424
Referencing the relevant part of the model's prior response before asking the next question makes

80
00:06:19.392 --> 00:06:25.632
the handoff explicit. Naming the current step in the workflow, for example, we are now revising

81
00:06:25.632 --> 00:06:32.192
the introduction, removes any ambiguity about what phase the conversation is in. These signals

82
00:06:32.192 --> 00:06:36.832
are small additions to each message, but they prevent the kind of drift that happens when

83
00:06:36.832 --> 00:06:39.312
model loses track of where it is in the future.

84
00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:43.920
multi-step workflow and starts treating each turn as if it were the first.

85
00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:51.360
Think of a multi-turn interaction as a series of turns that each carry a single job in order.

86
00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:57.040
Hold it that way in your mind and clarifying with the model stops feeling like an interruption.

87
00:06:57.040 --> 00:06:59.200
It becomes the turn that removes ambiguity.

88
00:06:59.328 --> 00:07:06.328
The structure holds as long as you pass prior output forward explicitly, instead of assuming

89
00:07:06.328 --> 00:07:09.488
the model still knows why it wrote that.

90
00:07:09.488 --> 00:07:11.128
That brings this one to a close.

91
00:07:11.128 --> 00:07:12.448
I'll see you in the next lesson.

